WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.169
- It's estimated that 75% of English words

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:04.963
come from other languages.

00:00:04.963 --> 00:00:07.007
It's a very grabby language,

00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:09.342
if it sees something
it likes, it takes it.

00:00:09.342 --> 00:00:12.637
That makes it kind of
the England of languages.

00:00:12.637 --> 00:00:14.723
That's one reason English has amassed

00:00:14.723 --> 00:00:17.767
one of the biggest vocabularies on earth.

00:00:17.767 --> 00:00:19.519
No one knows exactly how many,

00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:22.647
but it's surely in the
hundreds of thousands,

00:00:22.647 --> 00:00:25.900
and yet, there are still
gaps in the lexicon.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:29.237
There are just too many things
and ideas and experiences

00:00:29.237 --> 00:00:31.031
for one dictionary to hold.

00:00:31.031 --> 00:00:32.532
But hey, we can try.

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:35.910
We add almost 1,000 words a
year to the English language.

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:37.328
So if we're going to continue

00:00:37.328 --> 00:00:39.330
yoinking words from other languages,

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.915
I have a few suggestions.

00:00:40.915 --> 00:00:42.584
These are words that describe concepts

00:00:42.584 --> 00:00:44.169
that everyone can relate to,

00:00:44.169 --> 00:00:46.087
but we don't get to say in English.

00:00:46.087 --> 00:00:49.299
That's not to say that they're
entirely untranslatable,

00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:51.801
because we can do our best
approximation of them,

00:00:51.801 --> 00:00:52.927
but there's that intuition

00:00:52.927 --> 00:00:55.263
that a non-native speaker wouldn't have

00:00:55.263 --> 00:00:58.183
that, je ne sais quoi, if you will.

00:00:58.183 --> 00:00:59.768
That phrase is borrowed
from the French for,

00:00:59.768 --> 00:01:01.352
I don't know what,

00:01:01.352 --> 00:01:04.355
but now refers to that
little somethin' somethin'

00:01:04.355 --> 00:01:05.648
that's indefinable

00:01:05.648 --> 00:01:08.526
or je ne sais quoi.

00:01:08.526 --> 00:01:10.028
If you think my French is bad,

00:01:10.028 --> 00:01:12.405
just wait until you hear my Inuktitut.

00:01:12.405 --> 00:01:13.865
I'm Dr. Erica Brozovsky,

00:01:13.865 --> 00:01:15.867
and let's take a look at some other words,

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:19.120
because, after all, this is "Otherwords."

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:21.623
(funky music)

00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:28.379
- [Announcer] Otherwords.

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.716
- Some concepts are so universal,

00:01:31.716 --> 00:01:34.636
it's a surprise we don't
have words for them already.

00:01:34.636 --> 00:01:36.054
Here's a good example.

00:01:36.054 --> 00:01:36.888
What do you call it

00:01:36.888 --> 00:01:38.932
when you think of the
perfect witty comeback

00:01:38.932 --> 00:01:42.227
or snappy retort, but
hours after the fact,

00:01:42.227 --> 00:01:44.729
essentially, the opposite of a riposte?

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:46.147
English may leave you hanging,

00:01:46.147 --> 00:01:49.067
but Yiddish and German
and French all have a term

00:01:49.067 --> 00:01:51.152
for that blown mic drop opportunity.

00:01:51.152 --> 00:01:55.031
Trepverter, treppenwitz,
and l'esprit de l'escalier,

00:01:55.031 --> 00:01:58.034
which all roughly
translate to staircase wit,

00:01:58.034 --> 00:01:59.911
likely, because you
think of these comebacks

00:01:59.911 --> 00:02:00.870
when you're leaving,

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:02.413
walking down the staircase away

00:02:02.413 --> 00:02:03.248
from what could have been

00:02:03.248 --> 00:02:06.251
such a satisfying linguistic beatdown.

00:02:06.251 --> 00:02:08.294
And maybe you're so flustered,

00:02:08.294 --> 00:02:10.797
that on your way home you
ask someone for directions,

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:12.674
and say, "Yep, got it, thanks,"

00:02:12.674 --> 00:02:14.092
and get one block away

00:02:14.092 --> 00:02:16.678
and then immediately
forget what they said.

00:02:16.678 --> 00:02:19.264
Hawaiian's got a word for that, akihi.

00:02:19.264 --> 00:02:21.224
They also have a term, pana po'o,

00:02:21.224 --> 00:02:22.267
for scratching your head

00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:24.894
in order to remember
something you've forgotten,

00:02:24.894 --> 00:02:27.480
which might help with
remembering those directions.

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.941
Have you ever recklessly
taken a bite of food

00:02:29.941 --> 00:02:32.819
only to realize it's the
temperature of molten lava?

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:34.696
You don't wanna spit
it back onto your plate

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:35.989
in front of your date,

00:02:35.989 --> 00:02:38.700
and you're not about to
flambe your esophagus,

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.909
so your only choice is

00:02:39.909 --> 00:02:41.619
to keep it moving around in your mouth

00:02:41.619 --> 00:02:43.538
while sucking in air.

00:02:43.538 --> 00:02:46.666
Where English fails us, Buli,
a language spoken in Ghana,

00:02:46.666 --> 00:02:50.044
saves the day and maybe
our taste buds as well.

00:02:50.044 --> 00:02:53.590
Pelinti, literally means to
move hot food in your mouth,

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:54.799
and as an impatient eater,

00:02:54.799 --> 00:02:57.719
it happens to me more
often than I care to admit.

00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:00.972
We all try to listen to our
body when it's saying it's full,

00:03:00.972 --> 00:03:02.473
but, you know, sometimes,

00:03:02.473 --> 00:03:03.766
there's something left on your plate,

00:03:03.766 --> 00:03:05.894
and you wanna eat it
because it's delicious,

00:03:05.894 --> 00:03:07.896
but you don't wanna eat the whole thing,

00:03:07.896 --> 00:03:10.148
because you're not
actually hungry anymore,

00:03:10.148 --> 00:03:13.234
so you have a little spoonful
here and maybe a nibble there,

00:03:13.234 --> 00:03:15.612
and all of a sudden, it's gone.

00:03:15.612 --> 00:03:18.656
And by accident, you've become
part of the clean plate club.

00:03:18.656 --> 00:03:20.033
Not just me, right?

00:03:20.033 --> 00:03:20.867
Well, I know it's not,

00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:24.579
because Georgians only need
one word, shemomechama,

00:03:24.579 --> 00:03:28.082
to express what took me
79 words to describe.

00:03:28.082 --> 00:03:30.043
Speaking of mindless eating,

00:03:30.043 --> 00:03:32.795
Korean has a word for that
feeling of wanting to eat,

00:03:32.795 --> 00:03:35.173
not because you're particularly hungry,

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:39.010
but because your mouth is
bored, ib-i simsimhada.

00:03:39.010 --> 00:03:41.888
A more wistful way of
expressing a similar sentiment,

00:03:41.888 --> 00:03:44.349
the Japanese version, kuchisabishii,

00:03:44.349 --> 00:03:47.101
means to eat because your mouth is lonely.

00:03:47.101 --> 00:03:48.895
And then, when you've
satisfied your mouth,

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:51.439
and you're feeling a little sleepy,

00:03:51.439 --> 00:03:53.983
ah, there's an Italian
word for that, abbiocco.

00:03:53.983 --> 00:03:55.777
Like food, relationships

00:03:55.777 --> 00:03:58.196
are universally important to humans,

00:03:58.196 --> 00:04:01.115
so there are many words to
describe social interactions

00:04:01.115 --> 00:04:03.284
with special cultural nuance,

00:04:03.284 --> 00:04:06.371
and what better way to start
than around the dinner table?

00:04:06.371 --> 00:04:09.707
In Spanish culture, you don't
just chow down and get out.

00:04:09.707 --> 00:04:12.043
Sobremesa refers to the time you spend

00:04:12.043 --> 00:04:13.753
lingering around the table,

00:04:13.753 --> 00:04:16.297
enjoying your time
talking, drinking coffee,

00:04:16.297 --> 00:04:18.549
or a digestif for hours,

00:04:18.549 --> 00:04:21.135
with no rush to get onto the next thing,

00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:23.137
even at a busy restaurant.

00:04:23.137 --> 00:04:25.723
The Dutch have a word
for it too, natafelen.

00:04:25.723 --> 00:04:28.101
Many of us have heard of the Danish word,

00:04:28.101 --> 00:04:31.062
made famous by a certain book, "Hygge,"

00:04:31.062 --> 00:04:33.523
which highlights the custom
of cultivating an environment

00:04:33.523 --> 00:04:36.276
of warmth, coziness, and togetherness,

00:04:36.276 --> 00:04:38.945
which I imagine is important to prioritize

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:40.947
when it's dark and cold
for half of the year.

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:42.824
And speaking of togetherness,

00:04:42.824 --> 00:04:45.702
the Inuit people use piliriqatigiinniq

00:04:45.994 --> 00:04:47.578
to describe community spirit

00:04:47.578 --> 00:04:50.248
and working together for the common good.

00:04:50.248 --> 00:04:51.207
In warmer places,

00:04:51.207 --> 00:04:53.876
you might appreciate the time
after the sun has gone down

00:04:53.876 --> 00:04:56.379
a little bit more, so in Arabic,

00:04:56.379 --> 00:04:59.257
samar means staying up
late after the sun has set

00:04:59.257 --> 00:05:01.426
and enjoying time with friends.

00:05:01.426 --> 00:05:03.553
It also refers to evening conversations

00:05:03.553 --> 00:05:05.805
that might include
Arabic music and poetry.

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:09.559
I love how much meaning can
be packed into one word.

00:05:09.559 --> 00:05:11.561
Not that they're the
authority on the matter,

00:05:11.561 --> 00:05:14.230
but "The Guinness Book of World
Records" has weighed in on

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:16.107
what word does it best.

00:05:16.107 --> 00:05:17.358
The Yaghan people,

00:05:17.358 --> 00:05:20.445
whose last remaining speaker died in 2022,

00:05:20.445 --> 00:05:22.780
used mamihlapinatapai to describe

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.158
that meaningful look
shared between two people,

00:05:25.158 --> 00:05:27.076
who both wanna start something,

00:05:27.076 --> 00:05:29.412
but are unwilling or hesitant to do so,

00:05:29.412 --> 00:05:32.123
hoping that the other
person will initiate.

00:05:32.123 --> 00:05:35.084
Boy, that would save romcom
writers a lot of time.

00:05:35.084 --> 00:05:36.669
And as a non-native speaker

00:05:36.669 --> 00:05:39.088
of all the languages mentioned so far,

00:05:39.088 --> 00:05:40.798
just know that these explanations

00:05:40.798 --> 00:05:42.675
are simply scratching the surface.

00:05:42.675 --> 00:05:44.802
So, here's a word from my own idiolect

00:05:44.802 --> 00:05:48.765
that I found impossible to
translate exactly: máfan.

00:05:48.765 --> 00:05:50.391
A Mandarin dictionary will say it means

00:05:50.391 --> 00:05:54.020
troublesome or annoying, both
as an adjective and a verb,

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:56.230
but it's also so much more.

00:05:56.230 --> 00:05:58.483
I asked my mom how she
defined it in English,

00:05:58.483 --> 00:05:59.525
and she said.

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:03.071
- Máfan is inconvenient,
but I have to do it,

00:06:03.071 --> 00:06:05.656
like making this film for
my dear daughter, Erica.

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.826
- I also asked my Taiwanese
American community

00:06:08.826 --> 00:06:11.746
and got a wide range of answers,

00:06:11.746 --> 00:06:14.582
and I've used máfan in
all of those contexts.

00:06:14.582 --> 00:06:17.085
Language is complicated,
but that's half the fun.

00:06:17.085 --> 00:06:19.337
Whether you're looking for
a word to describe a joke,

00:06:19.337 --> 00:06:22.048
that's so unfunny that it's hilarious,

00:06:22.048 --> 00:06:23.883
or the feeling that overcomes you

00:06:23.883 --> 00:06:26.886
when you see something so cute

00:06:26.886 --> 00:06:28.596
that you can't help but squeeze it,

00:06:28.596 --> 00:06:31.015
there are countless
beautiful moments that exist

00:06:31.015 --> 00:06:33.643
outside of the limitations
of one language.

00:06:33.643 --> 00:06:34.727
There's a word in Hebrew

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:37.355
for the pure joy and
genuine happiness you feel

00:06:37.355 --> 00:06:38.856
about someone else's accomplishments

00:06:38.856 --> 00:06:40.691
with no ulterior motives.

00:06:40.691 --> 00:06:42.485
And you know the way sunlight filters

00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:43.903
through a canopy of trees,

00:06:43.903 --> 00:06:46.239
shining its dappled light on the ground?

00:06:46.239 --> 00:06:47.448
There's a Japanese word for it.

00:06:47.448 --> 00:06:50.410
By learning about these
so-called untranslatable words,

00:06:50.410 --> 00:06:51.494
you come to understand

00:06:51.494 --> 00:06:53.663
that though our dictionaries
may be different,

00:06:53.663 --> 00:06:57.250
the human experience is remarkably
similar across the globe.

00:06:58.626 --> 00:06:59.836
Mamihlapina,

00:06:59.836 --> 00:07:01.254
mamihlapinatapai,

00:07:02.088 --> 00:07:04.298
mamihlapinatapai, natapai,

00:07:04.298 --> 00:07:05.258
'cause it's like, ah, ah, ah.

00:07:05.258 --> 00:07:06.884
Inuk, it's iniktitut,

00:07:07.844 --> 00:07:09.679
or is it, inuktitut?

00:07:09.679 --> 00:07:12.515
I'm so proud of my
mispronunciations, guys.

